    "message": {
     "content": "### The Verdict: **Negotiate**\n\nThis is an **above-average opening offer** for an L6 Senior/Staff PM at Google in the Bay Area, but **you should not accept it as-is, nor should you walk away**. \n\nBecause Google recruited you out of a role where you are happy and well-compensated, you hold maximum leverage. You have clear room to improve the base, add a sign-on bonus, and slightly bump the equity.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Breaking Down the Numbers: Why the Offer is Better Than It Looks\n\nA critical detail with Google's offer is their **front-loaded equity vesting schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%)**. \n\nInstead of an even 25% per year, Google distributes the $1,080,000 GSU grant unevenly:\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | Vesting Equity | Total Comp (TC) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $235,000 | $47,000 | **$356,400** (33%) | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $235,000 | $47,000 | **$356,400** (33%) | **~$638,400** |\n| **Year 3** | $235,000 | $47,000 | **$237,600** (22%) | **~$519,600** + refreshes |\n| **Year 4** | $235,000 | $47,000 | **$129,600** (12%) | **~$411,600** + refreshes |\n\n* **Years 1 & 2:** At **~$638k/yr**, this is a substantial step up from your current **$526k/yr** at Meta.\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The Cliff):** Your unrefreshed compensation drops significantly. Annual performance refreshes (which vest over 4 years) are intended to backfill this gap, but as the recruiter noted, they are performance-dependent.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Gaps and Weak Points in the Current Offer\n\n1. **Lower Base Salary:** You currently make $240k base; Google offered $235k. The posted range maxes out at $271k, meaning they have ample headroom.\n2. **No Sign-on Bonus ($0):** It is standard practice in Big Tech to provide a sign-on bonus to bridge transition risk, cover missed bonus/vesting cycles, and offset the early-year differential.\n3. **Equity Ceiling:** $1.08M over 4 years (~$270k/yr annualized) is solid for L6, but strong L6 PM packages frequently land between **$1.15M and $1.25M**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nWhen countering, ask for adjustments across three specific buckets:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Counter Request | Target Landing Zone |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **4-Year RSUs** | $1,080,000 | **$1,200,000** | $1,120,000 \u2013 $1,160,000 |\n\n#### Why this structure works:\n* **Base ($255k):** Grounds your request in not taking a step backward from Meta\u2019s $240k while staying well within their $271k band limit.\n* **Sign-On ($75k):** Easy for recruiters to approve because it is a one-time budget line item and does not permanently inflate base or band equity limits.\n* **RSUs ($1.2M):** A modest ~11% bump that positions your Years 1 & 2 annual compensation closer to **$675k\u2013$700k**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Script the Negotiation\n\nMaintain a positive, collaborative tone. Emphasize that you were not actively looking and need the package to reflect the risk of leaving a stable, high-performing situation at Meta.\n\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting this together. I really enjoyed the team conversations and can clearly see the impact of this L6 scope.*\n> \n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a very strong spot at Meta, making a move needs to make unambiguous financial sense. While the equity grant is competitive, the base salary is slightly lower than my current $240k base, and there isn't a sign-on bonus to bridge the transition and forfeited near-term vesting.*\n> \n> *If we can bring the base salary to **$255,000**, include a **$75,000 sign-on bonus**, and adjust the equity grant to **$1,200,000**, this becomes an easy 'yes' for me and I\u2019d be excited to sign right away.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist\n* **Do not accept immediately:** Google almost never rescinds offers for polite, well-reasoned counter-offers from passive candidates.\n* **Focus on Total Year 1 & 2 Cash Flow:** Use the sign-on bonus and base adjustment to guarantee strong earnings upfront.\n* **Ask about Refresh Cadence:** Confirm when your first performance review and refresh cycle would take place to understand how quickly you can start stacking grants before the Year 3 cliff.",
